From: Magnus Lindholm <linmag7@gmail.com>
Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/1] sparc64: add shared Oberon MSI/MSI-X support

Enable bounded MSI/MSI-X support for recoverable endpoints sharing an
Oberon bridge. The four onboard BCM5718 ports on the M3000 share a bridge;
this version allows all four to allocate distinct vectors on one queue.
Generic PCI recovery and tg3 changes remain separate prerequisites.

The queue is stopped on delivery failure. Each affected driver is then
notified outside controller locks, so its recovery may release vectors
and reopen using legacy IRQs. A returned failure does not skip other devices; a stalled callback can
delay later notifications. Partially allocated MSI-X vectors may be released while function
masking remains enabled, without forcing unrelated devices into recovery.

The supported contract is deliberately bounded: one event queue per
bridge, 256 lifetime message IDs, no ID reuse, and published queue memory
retained until reboot. Exhaustion falls back to legacy interrupts.
CPU-hotplug, kexec and system-sleep configurations retain legacy IRQs.
The allocation is 1 MiB per initialized bridge, not per reopen. Message
IDs are consumed across reopen/rebind and failed setup, so indefinite MSI
operation through repeated allocation cycles is not supported. The fallback
preserves service but does not replenish the budget. Please review this
explicit capacity tradeoff as part of the first bounded implementation.

Queue readbacks are not treated as proof of DMA completion. Reclamation,
ID reuse, per-vector queue affinity and suspend/resume remain future work.
The architecture documentation specifies separate prerequisites for ID
reuse and storage reclamation. No available completion guarantee justifies
adding either in this revision; successful traffic tests are insufficient.
Bridge error-interrupt capture, clearing and rearming are not included.

Testing on an eight-CPU M3000:
- All four onboard ports allocated three distinct MSI-X vectors each.
- GLAN0 and GLAN2 passed simultaneous hashed 8 MiB transfers each way.
- GLAN2 close/reopen and further transfers passed.
- After 81 spare-port reopen cycles, ID exhaustion fell back to legacy
  IRQs without changing management's MSI-X vectors. Both paths then
  passed further hashed transfers, with taint zero.
- A separate diagnostic image deliberately stopped the shared MSI queue.
  All four devices were notified, all 12 vectors released, and all four
  reopened on legacy IRQs. Both connected ports passed transfers. The
  queue interrupt count stayed unchanged during those legacy transfers.

The stop fixture exercises containment and real driver recovery, not a
physical PCIe error or an actual queue overrun. It is not in this patch.
683 sanitizer-backed mocked callback/lifetime scenarios and ten endpoint
mask cases passed. UP/SMP W=1 links passed; UP was not booted. Subsequent
alignment cleanup and the disabled-in-test sleep guard produced identical
UP/SMP pci_fire objects. UP hibernation compilation/disassembly confirms
legacy admission. Existing unrelated full-build warnings remain.

The generic PCI API documents callback locking and binding changes. Its
review added no runtime code. The API still needs maintainer review. Actual runtime-PM,
AER, hot removal, firmware variants and arbitrary drivers are not claimed
qualified. Earlier single-device failure/overlap results are supporting
evidence, not additional four-device hardware tests.

Prerequisites: M3000 v5, the eight generic sparc64 MSI patches, four tg3
reset/removal fixes, the PCI/MSI recovery API and its tg3 consumer. The
standalone BCM5718 byte-order fix is included in the tested integration.

Codex assisted with implementation, review and test preparation.

